## Proof

**Yes**, such an operator exists. Consider the scalar operator $T = iI$ on $\ell^{2}(\mathbb{N})$, where $I$ is the identity operator and $i = \sqrt{-1}$.

**$T$ is bounded.** For every $x \in \ell^{2}(\mathbb{N})$,
$$\|Tx\| = \|iI x\| = |i|\,\|x\| = \|x\|,$$
so $\|T\| = 1 < \infty$.

**$T$ is normal.** The adjoint of $T = iI$ is $T^{*} = \overline{i}\,I = -iI$ (since for a scalar multiple of the identity, the adjoint is given by complex conjugation of the scalar). Then
$$TT^{*} = (iI)(-iI) = (-i^{2})\,I = I,$$
$$T^{*}T = (-iI)(iI) = (-i^{2})\,I = I.$$
Hence $TT^{*} = T^{*}T = I$, so $T$ is normal.

**$T$ is not self-adjoint.** We have $T^{*} = -iI \neq iI = T$. Therefore $T \neq T^{*}$, so $T$ is not self-adjoint.

Thus $T = iI$ is a bounded linear operator on $\ell^{2}(\mathbb{N})$ that is normal but not self-adjoint.

